    "temperature": {
      "file": "nitrogen_storage_vessel_temperature.glb",
      "title": "From −196 °C liquid to ambient gas",
      "description": "Liquid nitrogen boils at −196 °C (77 K). The vacuum-perlite annulus keeps heat leak so low that daily boil-off is only a fraction of a percent of capacity. Withdrawn liquid warms in the finned ambient vaporiser (heat from the air; frost forms on the cold lower tubes) and leaves close to ambient temperature. 1 litre of liquid gives ≈ 0.69 Nm³ of gas, so a closed-in liquid line must always have a relief device (trapped liquid → very high pressure).",
      "render": "normal",
      "annotations": []
    }
  },
